# =====================================================================
# Nagilai — native app configuration
# =====================================================================
# Only EXPO_PUBLIC_* variables reach the app bundle. Everything here is
# therefore public by definition — never put a secret in this file.
#
# Copy to `.env.local` for local development. For EAS builds these are
# set per profile in `eas.json`, or as EAS project secrets.
# =====================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backend
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where /api/v1 lives. The production value is set in eas.json.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Supabase project → Settings → API.
# The anon key is safe to ship: every table is protected by row level
# security, exactly as on the web. The service role key must NEVER
# appear in this file or anywhere in the mobile app.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sign in with Google — optional, and paired with Apple below
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# These are OAuth *client ids*, not secrets: they ship inside the app
# binary by design, which is why the EXPO_PUBLIC_ prefix is correct. The
# matching client *secret* goes only into the Supabase dashboard.
#
# Until they are set, the app hides the Google button entirely and email
# sign-in still works. No placeholder value will make it work — an
# invalid client id produces a rejected token, not a clearer error.
#
# Where to create them: Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services →
# Credentials → Create credentials → OAuth client ID. You need three,
# all in the same project:
#
#   1. Web application.  Authorised redirect URI:
#        https://<your-project>.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback
#      Its client id AND secret also go into the Supabase dashboard under
#      Authentication → Providers → Google.
#   2. iOS.        Bundle ID: com.nagilai.app
#   3. Android.    Package name: com.nagilai.app
#      SHA-1 fingerprint: `eas credentials` → Android → Keystore, or from
#      Play Console → Setup → App signing after the first upload.
#
# The web client id is required on every platform: Supabase validates the
# id token against it, so an iOS-only configuration produces a token
# Supabase then refuses.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=000000000000-xxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID=000000000000-yyyy.apps.googleusercontent.com
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_ANDROID_CLIENT_ID=000000000000-zzzz.apps.googleusercontent.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sign in with Apple — no environment variable, but not optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Apple sign-in needs no value here: the entitlement is declared in
# app.json (`ios.usesAppleSignIn`) and the app asks the OS at runtime
# whether it is available. What it does need, before it will work:
#
#   1. Apple Developer Program membership ($99/year).
#   2. The "Sign in with Apple" capability on the App ID for
#      com.nagilai.app (developer.apple.com → Identifiers).
#   3. A Services ID, a key, and the team id — all entered into the
#      Supabase dashboard under Authentication → Providers → Apple.
#
# App Store guideline 4.8 requires Apple sign-in wherever a third-party
# social login is offered. Google and Apple therefore ship together or
# not at all; `socialSignInReady()` in src/auth-providers.ts reports on
# the pair for exactly this reason.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Push notifications — no environment variable here either
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The app needs only the EAS project id, which `eas init` writes into
# app.json. Until that exists, `expoProjectId()` returns null, the app
# never asks for notification permission, and nothing pretends to work.
#
# The delivery credentials live outside this repository entirely:
#
#   iOS      — an APNs key, uploaded with `eas credentials`.
#   Android  — a Firebase service account JSON, uploaded the same way.
#   Server   — set EXPO_PUBLIC... no. On the *server*, set
#              EXPO_PUSH_ENABLED=true (see the root .env.example).
#              Without it the console provider runs and logs instead of
#              sending, which is how the whole path is testable today.
